{"type": "survey_submit", "survey_type": "post", "puzzle_idx": null, "answers": {"puzzle_1_rate_again": 3, "puzzle_1_guesses": "few", "puzzle_2_rate_again": 4, "puzzle_2_guesses": "never", "puzzle_3_rate_again": 5, "puzzle_3_guesses": "never", "puzzle_1_rating_reason": "There were a few bigger numbers (like the 10) that made it easier to colour squares in from the get go. This then helped me deduce which other squares should be coloured in as well. I did guess one square and luckily it was right lol", "puzzle_2_rating_reason": "It was harder than the first, mainly because all the numbers were relatively small. There were still enough rows where there is just one possible combination, making that a bit easier to fill in from the start.", "puzzle_3_rating_reason": "There were no rows that could be completely filled in from the start. All of the rows you could only at most fill in the squares that are always filled in regardless of the combination. It took the most thinking of the three, which is why I rated it the hardest.", "strategy": "First check for rows that only have one combination (ex. 10, 4 5, 3 6, etc.). These can be filled in automatically. Then, if any squares along the edges are filled in, that means any start/end numbers from the rows/columns can be automatically filled in as well (it's kinda hard to explain...). For some combinations, there are squares that will always be filled, so I filled those in (ex. if the row is just 6, then the 5th and 6th square in the row will always be filled no matter where the full 6 line is). ", "comments": "Not being used to the UI made it kind of hard. Specifically how when you multi-select any area, it automatically swaps the colouring of the square. So for the last puzzle, I think that feature made me not realize some of my squares got swapped. This then made me realize near the end that I had to restart since the squares weren't selected properly."}, "t": "2026-03-30T20:35:03.489671+00:00"}
